1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,043
White to move, and nothing is hanging.

2
00:00:02,163 --> 00:00:04,973
There is no tactic here, only a question:

3
00:00:05,093 --> 00:00:06,858
what is each side trying to do?

4
00:00:06,978 --> 00:00:08,290
The pawns tell you.

5
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:13,890
White has d4 and e3, Black has c6 and d5, and the c and e pawns are gone.

6
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:17,725
This is the Carlsbad structure, from the Queen's Gambit Exchange.

7
00:00:17,845 --> 00:00:20,062
Now look at the bishops through the pawns.

8
00:00:20,182 --> 00:00:25,836
Black's pawns sit on light squares, so the bishop on c8 is Black's bad bishop, locked in.

9
00:00:25,956 --> 00:00:28,104
White's bishop on d3 is the good one.

10
00:00:28,224 --> 00:00:31,057
Most of what follows comes from that one fact.

11
00:00:31,177 --> 00:00:36,053
Stockfish gives White about a third of a pawn, and five moves score within a tenth of each other.

12
00:00:36,173 --> 00:00:37,555
The move hardly matters.

13
00:00:37,675 --> 00:00:38,743
The plan does.

14
00:00:41,563 --> 00:00:43,827
Start with the most important line on the board.

15
00:00:43,947 --> 00:00:49,880
The queen on c2 and the bishop on d3 stand on the same diagonal, pointing at f5 and h7.

16
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,739
That battery decides what Black is allowed to do.

17
00:00:52,859 --> 00:00:57,724
Black's natural wish is to trade off the bad bishop on c8 with bishop to f5.

18
00:00:57,844 --> 00:01:00,027
Played right now, it drops a piece.

19
00:01:00,147 --> 00:01:04,106
White takes on f5, the knight recaptures, and the queen takes the knight.

20
00:01:04,226 --> 00:01:06,675
Knight to f5 fails the same way.

21
00:01:08,295 --> 00:01:13,543
And the knight on f6 cannot go hunting the bishop on f4, because it is the only guard of h7.

22
00:01:13,663 --> 00:01:16,775
Knight to h5 simply loses the h pawn with check.

23
00:01:17,595 --> 00:01:20,300
So Black's first job is one quiet pawn move:

24
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:21,198
g6.

25
00:01:21,318 --> 00:01:25,207
It covers f5 and blocks the diagonal to h7 in one go.

26
00:01:25,327 --> 00:01:31,155
After that, bishop to f5 is back on the menu, and the knight on d6 also gets f5 as a square.

27
00:01:31,275 --> 00:01:32,935
Here is Black's plan played out.

28
00:01:33,055 --> 00:01:37,815
Pawn to g6, bishop to f5, and the light squared bishops come off.

29
00:01:37,935 --> 00:01:43,322
The knight lands on f5, Black's worst piece is gone, and the evaluation drops to zero.

30
00:01:43,442 --> 00:01:46,217
That is the whole of Black's strategy in this structure.

31
00:01:46,337 --> 00:01:50,296
Everything else, like pawn to a5 against b4, is support.

32
00:01:52,616 --> 00:01:56,633
Black's knight on d6 deserves a look, because it cuts both ways.

33
00:01:56,753 --> 00:01:57,740
The good side:

34
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:01,355
it watches b5, c4, e4 and f5.

35
00:02:01,475 --> 00:02:07,860
That is exactly where White's queenside play wants to arrive, so the minority attack will need extra preparation.

36
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:09,060
The bad side:

37
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:13,197
it blocks its own bishop on e7, and it freezes the c pawn.

38
00:02:13,317 --> 00:02:18,994
Pawn to c5 would free Black's game, but pawn takes c5 hits the knight, and Black loses a pawn.

39
00:02:19,114 --> 00:02:21,773
So c6 stays where it is, a fixed target.

40
00:02:24,093 --> 00:02:25,312
Now White's plan.

41
00:02:25,432 --> 00:02:30,006
The c file is half open, so a rook goes to c1, aimed at c6.

42
00:02:30,126 --> 00:02:32,309
The square c5 is a weak spot:

43
00:02:32,429 --> 00:02:36,887
only pawn to b6 can chase a piece from it, and that loosens c6 even more.

44
00:02:37,007 --> 00:02:39,596
So a knight wants to go to a4 and then c5.

45
00:02:39,716 --> 00:02:41,771
First, though, pawn to h3.

46
00:02:41,891 --> 00:02:43,238
It is not a waiting move.

47
00:02:43,358 --> 00:02:48,606
It takes g4 from the black bishop, and it clears h2 as a retreat for White's bishop.

48
00:02:48,726 --> 00:02:52,534
Without it, knight to h5 forces White to give up that bishop.

49
00:02:52,654 --> 00:02:56,021
With it, the bishop drops back to h2 and stays.

50
00:02:56,141 --> 00:02:59,589
Then rook to c1, knight to a4, knight to c5.

51
00:02:59,709 --> 00:03:01,926
The other lever is the minority attack:

52
00:03:02,046 --> 00:03:05,053
the a and b pawns march against three black pawns.

53
00:03:05,173 --> 00:03:09,794
Because the d6 knight guards b5, White needs pawn to a4 first.

54
00:03:09,914 --> 00:03:16,915
Then pawn to b5 hits c6, and after the exchanges Black is left with a weak pawn on c6 or on d5.

55
00:03:17,735 --> 00:03:19,221
What White should not do.

56
00:03:19,341 --> 00:03:27,282
The battery tempts bishop takes h7 check, but with no knight able to reach g5 there is no follow-up, and White is just a piece down.

57
00:03:27,402 --> 00:03:29,492
Pawn to g4 just loses the pawn.

58
00:03:29,612 --> 00:03:36,532
Pawn to e4 right now opens the centre while Black is fully developed, and White ends up with an isolated pawn on d4.

59
00:03:36,652 --> 00:03:39,960
And bishop takes d6 gives Black the two bishops for nothing.

60
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:43,227
Each of these costs between half a pawn and two pawns.

61
00:03:44,047 --> 00:03:46,079
So the position is about two squares.

62
00:03:46,199 --> 00:03:49,542
Black wants f5, to trade the bad bishop and plant a knight.

63
00:03:49,662 --> 00:03:52,611
White wants c5, and a queenside pawn break.

64
00:03:52,731 --> 00:03:58,792
White cannot stop the bishop trade once g6 is played, so White's edge is small, a third of a pawn.

65
00:03:58,912 --> 00:04:00,096
The rules for White:

66
00:04:00,216 --> 00:04:06,079
pawn to h3, rooks to c1, knight to c5, pawns to b4 and a4, then b5.

67
00:04:06,199 --> 00:04:07,604
The rules for Black:

68
00:04:07,724 --> 00:04:14,376
g6 before any piece touches f5, then bishop to f5, and pawn to a5 to slow the minority attack.

